    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that overview. Could you go into a bit more detail regarding the specific enzyme involved in this cascade? I'd like to explain to my students exactly how the cell wall rupture triggers the enzymatic reaction, and perhaps how the transition from sulfoxides to the volatile S-oxide happens so rapidly once the onion is cut.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The key enzyme initiating this dramatic chemical release is myrosinase, which is stored separately from the glucosinolates within the onion cells. When the cell walls rupture during slicing, myrosinase comes into contact with its substrate, the glucosinolates. This contact triggers the hydrolysis of the glucosinolates, a reaction that is remarkably fast, often occurring in a near-instantaneous burst, which is why the pungency is so immediate.\n\nThe transition to volatile S-oxides, like propanethial S-oxide, involves further enzymatic or spontaneous breakdown of the initial products, such as thiocyanates, which can then be oxidized. This rapid oxidation step is what generates the highly reactive, volatile sulfur compounds that cause the stinging sensation in the eyes. It's a finely tuned, yet chaotic, cellular defense mechanism.",
      "source": "target",
